# task-cancel - cancel is acknowledged and never rewrites a terminal state.
#
# VALIDATES: spec-mcp2026-3-tasks-extension AC-9. Cancellation is cooperative:
# the server acknowledges it and does not guarantee the task stops, and a task
# that already reached a terminal state stays in the state it reached.
#
# WHAT CHANGED AND WHY: the pre-cutover file called `task-call ze_monitor_bgp`,
# which sent `task:{}` to opt the call into task execution. That opt-in is gone
# (D-1): the SERVER decides from the `ze:task-support required` annotation
# ze_monitor_bgp carries, so the identical directive now sends no task member
# and the handle comes back unasked. The file also asserted only
# `contains=completed`, which the `task-wait` line already guarantees before
# cancel is ever sent -- so the cancel half was unasserted. It is asserted now:
# the state AFTER the cancel is read back and must still be `completed`.
#
# The idempotence claim is the substance here. A cancel that overwrote the
# terminal state would silently destroy a completed task's result, and the old
# assertion could not see it.
#
# WHY THE TASK IS ALREADY TERMINAL, AND WHERE THE RUNNING CASE IS PROVED: no
# `ze:task-support required` command blocks. Every one of them (monitor bgp,
# monitor ping, monitor interface rate, subscribe, show rib) answers its RPC
# from a snapshot and returns, so a `task-cancel` issued straight after
# `task-call` would race the worker and land on `cancelled` or `completed`
# depending on host load -- a timing assumption, which is the defect
# ai/rules/fix-dont-record.md bans rather than a test. Cancelling a genuinely
# WORKING task is proved deterministically instead by
# TestTasksCancelAcknowledgesWithAnEmptyResult/working_task
# (internal/component/mcp/tasks_test.go), which drives the same HTTP transport
# with a dispatcher blocked on ctx.Done(), so the worker is provably still
# running when the cancel arrives, and asserts the state becomes `cancelled`.
# This file owns the two properties a functional run CAN pin deterministically:
# the empty acknowledgement, and terminal-state idempotence.

stdin=mcp:terminator=EOF_MCP
# ze_monitor_bgp is GENERATED from the command registry and carries
# `ze:task-support required`. Waiting for it proves the registry is populated;
# waiting on a handcrafted tool would not, since those are served from a static
# list and appear immediately.
wait-tool ze_monitor_bgp
# No `task` member: the server creates the task from the annotation. task-call
# fails the run unless the reply is resultType "task" with a taskId, so the
# server-directed creation is enforced here rather than merely assumed.
task-call ze_monitor_bgp {}
task-wait $LAST completed
# Cancel a task that is ALREADY terminal: acknowledged, and a no-op.
task-cancel $LAST
# Read the state back. This is the assertion the old file was missing.
task-get $LAST
EOF_MCP

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
bgp {
	peer peer1 {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 1
				remote 1
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

option=tcp_connections:value=0

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze --mcp $PORT2 -:stdin=ze-bgp
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze-test mcp --port $PORT2 --tasks:stdin=mcp:timeout=15s
expect=exit:code=0
# The acknowledgement was EMPTY. task-cancel prints this line only after
# requireEmptyAck (internal/test/cli/cmd_mcp_calls.go) has confirmed the result
# carries nothing beyond resultType and _meta; a server volunteering a taskId or
# a status fails the client instead, which is what the stderr reject below
# names. MCP 2026-07-28 ext-tasks: "Acknowledge cancellation requests with an
# empty result."
expect=stdout:contains=task-cancel acknowledged task=
# The state after the cancel. task-get prints the status, so this line reads the
# POST-cancel value rather than the one task-wait already proved.
expect=stdout:contains=completed
# The cancel did not rewrite the terminal state. A cancel applied to an already
# completed task must not move it to `cancelled`.
reject=stdout:contains=cancelled
# The acknowledgement did not grow a payload. This replaces a
# `reject=stdout:contains=code -32021` that could never match: probe prints
# `code=<n>` (cmd_mcp.go formatProbeResult) and the space-separated form exists
# only in a client error string, which goes to stderr -- and this file sends no
# probe at all, so stdout never carries a `code=` token in either spelling.
reject=stderr:pattern=acknowledgement is not empty
reject=stderr:pattern=code \-32021
reject=stderr:pattern=code \-32602
